  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 AM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nDense fog has developed over Nebraska and northern Kansas early this \nmorning and could spread southward towards I-70 corridor. Hot and \nhumid conditions along with dry weather will be on tap across the \nregion for today and Friday with upper level high pressure remaining \nin control over the area. Heading into the weekend models in good \nagreement with a upper level wave projected to eject from the \nRockies out into the central plains on Sunday. This system will \npush a frontal boundary slowly southward on Saturday with \nanother hot day expected for locations south of the front. \nScattered thunderstorms look to develop along the front Saturday\nafternoon and could become more numerous Saturday night as the \nfront slows down. A few strong storms are possible with severe \nweather chances low due to weak wind shear. There is some \npotential for heavy rainfall given higher precipitable water \nvalues and slower storm movement. Models show the front sinking \nslowly southward from Kansas into Oklahoma by Sunday night. This\nwill keep decent chances for rain over the area on Sunday into \nSunday night along with cooler temperatures finally overspreading\nthe area.\n\nHeading into next week model ensembles continue to show high \nprobabilities of daytime highs remaining below 85 degrees across\nmuch of the area.",
